# Frees the undefined constants of the sources before any pass that can act
# on them. A script runs it right after `hierarchy -top <top>`, once every
# source is read and every parameter set, and lists this file in [files].
#
# proc runs pass by pass, without proc_rom, and its closing opt_expr runs last.
# The first setundef frees the undefined inputs of every multiplexer and every
# undefined bit of a connection, before proc_dlatch, which reads both (the
# connections through its signal map) and would make an all-x case in a
# latch's fan-in hold. It selects no memory, since setundef reads a selected
# memory's initialization, which processes still drive until proc_dlatch, and
# no process. The second frees every other undefined bit, in cells,
# connections and the processes that are left, before proc_dff, which would
# otherwise store an undefined asynchronous reset value in a register's
# parameters; a free reset value becomes an asynchronous load instead. The
# opt_expr between them writes the constants of memory initializations into
# their cells, which setundef's memory handling requires, and changes no other
# cell. The last opt_expr is proc's own closing step, run once no undefined
# constant is left for it: it folds what plain proc folds, such as a
# multiplexer with a constant select, so that the commands after the opening
# see the netlist they saw.
proc_clean
proc_rmdead
proc_prune
proc_init
proc_arst
proc_mux
setundef -anyseq t:$mux t:$pmux t:$bwmux w:*
proc_dlatch
opt_expr -keepdc t:$meminit*
setundef -anyseq
proc_dff
proc_memwr
proc_clean
opt_expr -keepdc
